      'A reflective shadow map (Dachsbacher & Stamminger 2005) is a shadow map that also remembers what it saw. Next to depth, the sun\'s pass writes each texel\'s world normal and its reflected flux: albedo × sun irradiance (for a directional light every texel catches the same flux, so the texel area cancels out). World positions come back from depth. Every lit texel is then a tiny area light, and a receiver gathers N of them from a disc around its own position in the light\'s view, each weighted by cos θp · cos θx / d². Sample radii grow linearly with a stratified ξ, so they crowd toward the centre where nearby texels matter most, and each is weighted by ξ to compensate (the paper\'s importance distribution).',
      'The gather is the expensive part, so it runs on a quarter-resolution G-buffer (position and normal). A separable cross-bilateral blur that stops at normal and plane edges removes most of the noise. The main pass then upsamples (the paper\'s trick): it averages the four nearest low-resolution results whose position and normal match the pixel, and where fewer than three match (silhouettes, table legs) it gathers again at full resolution with half the samples. The Upsample fallback view paints those pixels magenta. The disc rotates per pixel and per frame, and TAA averages what noise is left. Bilinear upsampling, for comparison, bleeds light across every edge.',
      'What RSM ignores: occlusion. The gather never asks whether anything lies between a texel and the receiver, so the rug\'s red glow reaches the sofa side of the screen and the back wall behind it, which Irradiance Probes (with visibility) keeps dark. It is also one bounce from one light: texels in shadow never reflect, the sky is a flat fill here, and reflectors farther than the sampling radius (in the light\'s view) are missed, while a wide radius with few samples is noisy. Indoors, where the sun lights a small patch, most samples land on texels that face away or are dark, which is the hardest case for RSM.',
